# Composing Collectives Above a Black-Box Vendor Library on AWS Trainium

**TL;DR**: Agent loop discovers per-collective compositions that beat the
developer-written baseline at multi-node training scale by **1.40×** (OLMoE-10B
e2e) and **3.04–6.48×** (Llama amp1–amp4 e2e, M=4 → M=8) — with per-primitive
per-step speedups of **4–12×** across primitives, all exceeding the e2e
headline because constant non-collective compute (attention, FFN matmul)
dilutes the ratio. The default strategy-enumerate search style covers the
8 collective problems in **56 wall-minutes** at **~$19 of Sonnet 4.5
tokens**; cc-react and multi-island ablations cost a comparable amount per
style and all three converge to within <0.5% on OLMoE step time and ~10%
on Llama amp bundled step time.

Profiling-driven search for fast composite collectives on AWS Trainium
(trn1.32xlarge). The vendor's collective library is treated as an oracle
— we never modify it and never see inside it; we only call its primitives
(`xm.all_gather`, `xm.reduce_scatter`, `xm.all_reduce`, `xm.collective_permute`,
`xm.all_to_all`) and pre/post-process the data with local XLA ops.

Within that layer the agent searches over compositions: how many primitive
dispatches, in what order, against what tensor layout, with what local
work in between. A simulator built from on-device profiling ranks
candidates; an LLM proposes new candidates; the top survivors are
benchmarked on real Trainium and validated end-to-end in OLMoE-10B
training. Search results are emitted as `runtime/trainium_<problem>.py`
modules that drop into a real training loop.

## Why this framing, and what it is *not*

This is not a competitor to MSCCL/TACCL, AlphaEvolve, or FunSearch.

| Prior work | What they assume / target | Why they don't apply here |
|---|---|---|
| **MSCCL / TACCL** | Programmable schedules, open primitives (NCCL/MSCCL-IR), explicit chunking and permutation steps | Trainium's collective library is closed; we cannot rewrite the schedule below the `xm.*` boundary |
| **AlphaEvolve / FunSearch** | Search the algorithm space — invent new algorithms (matrix multiplication, hashing, sort) | We're not looking for new algorithms; we're looking for the right way to *compose* a small fixed primitive set against a black-box backend |
| **AutoTVM / Triton autotune** | Search the kernel implementation space (loop tiling, vectorization, schedule) | We never write kernels; the backend writes the kernel for each primitive call |

The contribution is a *closed-stack* search loop: an empirical, on-device
profiling layer + a cost model that captures vendor-library quirks
(per-graph NEFF compilation cost, per-mark_step overhead, per-op
kernel-launch floor, contiguity-aware implicit-copy charging) + an LLM
that proposes compositions, all gated through correctness tests against
a pure-Python reference and an end-to-end MoE training validation step.
Reviewers cannot ask "why LLM vs. classical search" because the LLM is
not the contribution — the *loop* is. They cannot ask "why not compare
to MSCCL" because MSCCL operates at a layer we can't access on this
hardware. The only honest comparison is *against the developer-written
baseline a practitioner would write inside a real training script*,
which is what we report.

## Eight Collective Problems

Four OLMoE-side collectives and four Llama-side composable primitives.
All eight are registered as `CollectiveProblem` entries the agent
search loops over.

| Problem | Use Case | Agent's Composition |
|---|---|---|
| **AllToAllV** | Variable-length MoE dispatch/combine | pack + 1 `all_gather` + `index_select` |
| **Uniform AllToAll** | Fixed-capacity MoE token exchange | 1 `all_gather` + per-source slice + `cat` |
| **Ring Attention KV** | KV cache distribution for seq-parallel | one `all_gather` per slot (K, V) — 2 dispatches per call |
| **Distributed Cross-Entropy (dxe)** | Loss on vocab-sharded logits without materializing full `(B·S, V)` | 2 `all_reduce`s: one on local `Σ exp(logits)`, one on per-token target logit (bf16-safe, no max-shift) |
| **PP cross-stage send/recv** | Llama pipeline-parallel activation transfer | M activations stacked into one `(M, B, S, DM)` buffer, 1 masked `all_reduce` (replaces M per-mb ARs) |
| **TP MLP** | Tensor-parallel SwiGLU MLP | per-(m, L) partials stacked via `torch.stack`, 1 fused `all_reduce` over the bundled `(M, L, B, S, DM)` buffer (replaces M·L per-microbatch ARs) |
| **FSDP weight prefetch** | Per-microbatch sharded-weight `all_gather` | per-microbatch shards stacked per layer into `(M, *shard_shape)`, 1 fused `all_gather` per layer (replaces M per-microbatch AGs) |
| **Layer block AR** | Within-layer attn+MLP AR fusion (PaLM/GPT-J parallel-attn) | `attn_partial` and `mlp_partial` stacked into a single `(2, B, S, DM)` buffer, 1 fused AR (replaces the two sequential per-sub-block ARs) |

The default Phase-3 search loop is **strategy-enumerate** (LLM
enumerates K candidate strategies in one prompt, then refines each
in its own short trajectory; `--phase3-style strategy-enumerate`).
The paper's ablation section reports two alternative loops with
the same Phase-1/4/5 mechanics:

- **cc-react** — single-trajectory ReAct over K × max_rounds turns
  with full prior history (`--phase3-style cc-react`).
- **multi-island** — K-island GA with crossover
  (`--phase3-style multi-island`).

All three converge to within **0.5% on the OLMoE-10B step time**
(end-to-end 7-node spread 3198.6 / 3216.1 / 3215.4 ms, i.e.
1.40× / 1.39× / 1.39×). Strategy-enumerate is the cheapest of the
three; see the cost-per-style table in the paper's Ablation Study
section (Table 9).

## Reproducing paper Tables 2-6 + Figure 3

Prerequisite: 7-node trn1.32xlarge cluster, master at `$MASTER`, workers
at `$WORKERS` (comma-separated private IPs), repo cloned to
`/home/ubuntu/agentic-collective-communication`, venv at
`/opt/aws_neuronx_venv_pytorch_2_9`. Set `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in
`/home/ubuntu/.anthropic_env` to run searches.

### 1. Reproduce search (Tables 2–6 + Fig 3 all depend on the deployed runtime/ files)

Pick one stack or run all three for the ablation.

```bash
for STYLE in strategy-enumerate cc-react multi-island; do
  for P in tp_mlp fsdp_prefetch pp_send_recv llama_block_ar \
           dxe ring_kv uniform_a2a alltoallv; do
    python3 experiments/run_search.py --problem $P --phase3-style $STYLE \
      --max-rounds 4 --num-nodes 7 \
      --master-addr $MASTER --worker-addrs $WORKERS \
      --output-dir results/search/$STYLE/$P
  done
done
```

Token usage lands in `/tmp/token_usage.jsonl` (one record per
search). Per-style cost over the 8 collective problems is **~$19**
in Sonnet 4.5 list-price tokens (per-search average ~$2.40).
Per-style wall-time on the 7-node cluster: strategy-enumerate
**56 min**, cc-react **75 min**, multi-island **78 min**.

### 2. Reproduce Table 3 (OLMoE end-to-end 7-node speedup)

Use **static** rdzv backend (more reliable at 224 ranks than c10d):

Baseline:

```bash
PERCALL_PROBE=1 PROBE_START_STEP=200 PROBE_END_STEP=230 \
    torchrun --nproc_per_node=32 --nnodes=7 \
    --rdzv_backend=static --master_addr=$MASTER --master_port=29500 \
    training/train_olmoe10b.py \
    --backend baseline --grad-sync baseline --ce baseline \
    --steps 250 --warmup 30
```

Agent (after deploying runtime files from step 1; --grad-sync baseline
forces per-tensor AR for cross-stack consistency):

```bash
PERCALL_PROBE=1 PROBE_START_STEP=200 PROBE_END_STEP=230 \
    torchrun --nproc_per_node=32 --nnodes=7 \
    --rdzv_backend=static --master_addr=$MASTER --master_port=29500 \
    training/train_olmoe10b.py \
    --backend agent --grad-sync baseline --ce agent \
    --steps 250 --warmup 30
```

Steady-state numbers (unprobed steady, dropping probe window): baseline 4412.9 ms →
agent 3123.5 ms = **1.41×**. Probed-window numbers (3 stacks): 1.30× (probe
overhead dilutes; the unprobed value is the production-relevant headline).
Per-call probes record both `*_wall` (entry-to-exit wall clock) and
device-only (`wait_device_ops()` bracket) variants — see Section 8 below.

### 2b. Reproduce Llama-7B-equivalent end-to-end (paper Table tab:llama7b)

Edit the shape constants in
`experiments/model_extension/train_llama_e2e_7b.py` (or generate via
the sed-recipe in `scripts/r28_llama7b_setup.sh`):
DM=4096, HID=14336, N_LAYERS_PER_STAGE=16 (32 total at PP=2),
SEQLEN=2048, M=4, VOCAB=224*144=32256 sharded.

```bash
# baseline (per-microbatch developer pattern)
torchrun --nproc_per_node=32 --nnodes=7 \
    --rdzv_backend=static --master_addr=$MASTER --master_port=29701 \
    experiments/model_extension/train_llama_e2e_7b.py per_mb 200

# agent (strategy-enum bundled stack)
torchrun --nproc_per_node=32 --nnodes=7 \
    --rdzv_backend=static --master_addr=$MASTER --master_port=29702 \
    experiments/model_extension/train_llama_e2e_7b.py bundled 200
```

Steady median (200 warmup-dropped steps): per_mb 71.3 ms / bundled
22.0 ms = **3.24×**.

